\title{Monotone Common-Key Couplings for Finite Site and Bond Percolation}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2 August 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We present a deterministic common-key construction that simultaneously generates finite percolation configurations at every real threshold. Each vertex in a site model, or each edge in a bond model, receives one real-valued key and is declared open at level $p$ exactly when its key is at most $p$. The configurations are pointwise nested: if $p\le q$, every object open at $p$ remains open at $q$. We prove that site connectivity and bond connectivity persist on arbitrary graphs, establish persistence of horizontal site crossings in every positive finite square grid, and combine the site and bond conclusions without conflating their distinct sample spaces. The proofs require neither independence nor a probability measure. For independent uniform keys, the construction couples all Bernoulli percolation parameters and yields monotonicity of probabilities of increasing events. We also describe event-driven algorithms, applications, limitations, and directions toward sharp and infinite-volume results.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
Percolation studies connectivity in a medium whose components are open or closed. In site percolation the components are vertices; in bond percolation they are edges. Opening more components cannot destroy an existing open path. Yet independently sampled configurations at two densities do not display this pointwise order: a lower-density sample may happen to cross while an unrelated higher-density sample does not.

A coupling resolves the mismatch by constructing both samples from shared randomness. Assign one permanent numerical key to every component. At threshold $p$, exactly those components with key at most $p$ are open. The same keys generate all thresholds, and the family grows by inclusion. We isolate the deterministic content: exact membership, pointwise nesting, persistence of site and bond connectivity, persistence of horizontal grid crossings, and simultaneous treatment of site and bond models on their separate state spaces.

These finite statements need no random law. Their probabilistic consequences appear only after the order structure is established. They are not, by themselves, infinite-volume critical-threshold theorems; rather, they provide the canonical comparison across parameters on which stronger analysis can build.

\section{Graphs and configurations}
Let $G=(V,E)$ be a simple undirected graph, with edges understood as unordered pairs of distinct vertices. A finite path from $u$ to $v$ is a sequence $u=x_0,x_1,\ldots,x_k=v$ in which each $\{x_{i-1},x_i\}$ belongs to $E$.

\begin{definition}[Site configuration]
A site configuration is a map $\eta:V\to\{0,1\}$. A vertex is open when its value is $1$. A path is an open site path when all of its vertices, including its endpoints, are open. Vertices are site-connected when an open site path joins them.
\end{definition}

Write $\eta\preceq\eta'$ when $\eta(x)=1$ implies $\eta'(x)=1$ for every $x\in V$.

\begin{definition}[Bond configuration]
A bond configuration is a map $\omega:E\to\{0,1\}$. An edge is open when its value is $1$. A path is an open bond path when all its traversed edges are open. Vertices are bond-connected when such a path joins them.
\end{definition}

Write $\omega\preceq\omega'$ when $\omega(e)=1$ implies $\omega'(e)=1$ for every $e\in E$. Site and bond configurations are different objects: the former are indexed by vertices and the latter by edges.

\section{Common-key threshold configurations}
\begin{definition}[Site threshold configuration]
Given keys $K:V\to\mathbb R$ and $p\in\mathbb R$, define
\[
\eta_p^K(x)=\mathbf 1\{K(x)\le p\}.
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Bond threshold configuration]
Given keys $L:E\to\mathbb R$ and $p\in\mathbb R$, define
\[
\omega_p^L(e)=\mathbf 1\{L(e)\le p\}.
\]
\end{definition}

The threshold may be any real number. The interval $[0,1]$ becomes relevant only for uniform keys.

\begin{lemma}[Exact threshold membership]
For every vertex $x$, edge $e$, and threshold $p$,
\[
\eta_p^K(x)=1\Longleftrightarrow K(x)\le p,
\qquad
\omega_p^L(e)=1\Longleftrightarrow L(e)\le p.
\]
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
These are the defining cases of the two indicator functions.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Pointwise threshold nesting]
If $p\le q$, then
\[
\eta_p^K\preceq\eta_q^K
\qquad\text{and}\qquad
\omega_p^L\preceq\omega_q^L.
\]
Equivalently,
\[
\{x:K(x)\le p\}\subseteq\{x:K(x)\le q\},
\quad
\{e:L(e)\le p\}\subseteq\{e:L(e)\le q\}.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
If $x$ is open at $p$, then $K(x)\le p\le q$, so it is open at $q$. The edge argument is identical.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
At equal thresholds the configurations agree. Repeated keys cause no ambiguity: all objects with key exactly $p$ enter at threshold $p$.
\end{remark}

\section{Persistence of connectivity}
\begin{theorem}[Site connectivity persistence]
Let $K:V\to\mathbb R$ and $p\le q$. If $u$ and $v$ are site-connected in $\eta_p^K$, then they are site-connected in $\eta_q^K$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Choose an open site path $u=x_0,\ldots,x_k=v$ at $p$. Pointwise nesting keeps every $x_i$ open at $q$, while graph adjacency is unchanged. The same path is therefore a witness at $q$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Bond connectivity persistence]
Let $L:E\to\mathbb R$ and $p\le q$. If $u$ and $v$ are bond-connected in $\omega_p^L$, then they are bond-connected in $\omega_q^L$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Every edge of a witness path has key at most $p$, hence at most $q$. The same path remains open.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Increasing event]
An event $A$ on site configurations is increasing if $\eta\preceq\eta'$ and $\eta\in A$ imply $\eta'\in A$. The bond definition is analogous.
\end{definition}

Connectivity of fixed endpoints is increasing. Other examples include the existence of a cluster of prescribed minimum size, a route between two boundary sets, or several disjoint routes.

\begin{corollary}[Pointwise event monotonicity]
For an increasing site event $A$ and $p\le q$,
\[
\mathbf 1_A(\eta_p^K)\le\mathbf 1_A(\eta_q^K).
\]
The analogous conclusion holds for increasing bond events.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Apply the definition of an increasing event to pointwise threshold nesting.
\end{proof}

\section{Horizontal crossings of finite grids}
For $n>0$, let $V_n=\{0,\ldots,n-1\}^2$, with two vertices adjacent when their Manhattan distance is one. Define
\[
L_n=\{(0,j):0\le j<n\},\qquad
R_n=\{(n-1,j):0\le j<n\}.
\]

\begin{definition}[Horizontal site crossing]
A site configuration on $V_n$ has a horizontal crossing when an open site path begins in $L_n$ and ends in $R_n$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Horizontal crossing persistence]
Let $K:V_n\to\mathbb R$ and $p\le q$. If $\eta_p^K$ has a horizontal crossing, then $\eta_q^K$ has a horizontal crossing.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The lower-threshold crossing gives boundary endpoints and an open path. Site connectivity persistence preserves that path, and its endpoints remain on the same boundaries.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Realization-wise crossing threshold]
For fixed keys on a finite grid, the indicator
\[
H_K(p)=\mathbf 1\{\eta_p^K\text{ has a horizontal crossing}\}
\]
is a nondecreasing step function. If a crossing occurs, there is a least key level
\[
p_*(K)=\min\{K(x):H_K(K(x))=1\},
\]
and $H_K(p)=0$ below $p_*(K)$ while $H_K(p)=1$ at and above it.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Persistence gives monotonicity. A finite configuration changes only at finitely many key values, so a first successful key exists.
\end{proof}

This sample-dependent value is not an infinite-volume critical probability.

\section{Simultaneous site and bond persistence}
\begin{theorem}[Joint site-and-bond connectivity persistence]
Let $G_s=(V,E_s)$ have vertex keys $K:V\to\mathbb R$, and let $G_b=(V,E_b)$ have edge keys $L:E_b\to\mathbb R$. Choose site endpoints $s_u,s_v$ and bond endpoints $b_u,b_v$. If $p\le q$, if $s_u,s_v$ are site-connected at $p$, and if $b_u,b_v$ are bond-connected at $p$, then both connections hold at $q$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Apply site connectivity persistence to the first connection and bond connectivity persistence to the second, then combine the conclusions.
\end{proof}

The theorem keeps the two state spaces distinct. A common order principle does not identify vertex states with edge states or equate the global models.

\section{Probabilistic specialization}
Suppose $V$ is finite and the keys $K(x)$ are independent and uniform on $[0,1]$. For $p\in[0,1]$,
\[
\mathbb P(\eta_p^K(x)=1)=\mathbb P(K(x)\le p)=p.
\]
Independence of keys yields independent site states, so each snapshot has the Bernoulli site-percolation law with parameter $p$.

\begin{proposition}[Finite configuration probability]
For a prescribed site configuration $\eta$, set
\[
N_1(\eta)=|\{x:\eta(x)=1\}|,
\qquad
N_0(\eta)=|\{x:\eta(x)=0\}|.
\]
Then
\[
\mathbb P(\eta_p^K=\eta)=p^{N_1(\eta)}(1-p)^{N_0(\eta)}.
\]
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Each required open site contributes $p$, each required closed site contributes $1-p$, and independence turns their intersection into the product.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Probability monotonicity]
For every increasing event $A$ on a finite site set, $p\mapsto\mathbb P(\eta_p^K\in A)$ is nondecreasing on $[0,1]$. The bond analogue holds under independent uniform edge keys.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
The event indicators are pointwise ordered at $p\le q$. Taking expectations preserves the inequality.
\end{proof}

Strict monotonicity is stronger: it requires a positive-probability set of key assignments on which the event changes between the two parameters.

\section{Algorithms}
\subsection{Direct evaluation}
For $m$ keyed objects, constructing the threshold configuration takes $O(m)$ comparisons. Breadth-first or depth-first search tests connectivity in $O(|V|+|E|)$ time. On an $n\times n$ grid, horizontal crossing detection starts from all open left-boundary sites and succeeds upon reaching the right boundary, using $O(n^2)$ time and memory in the worst case.

\subsection{Event-driven sweep}
For many threshold queries, sort all keys once in $O(m\log m)$ time and activate objects in nondecreasing order. For site connectivity, union each newly activated vertex with active neighbors. For bond connectivity, union the endpoints of each newly activated edge. Path compression and union by rank give $O(m\alpha(m))$ total union--find overhead after sorting, where $\alpha$ is the inverse Ackermann function.

For grid crossings, introduce virtual left and right boundary nodes. Connect each active boundary vertex to its virtual node. The first activation level at which the virtual nodes share a representative is $p_*(K)$.

\subsection{Coupled Monte Carlo}
To estimate crossing probabilities at $p_1<\cdots<p_r$, sample one key field per trial and evaluate all thresholds from it. Every marginal is correct and each trial's indicators are nested. Paired indicators directly estimate probability increments and often reduce variance compared with independent resampling, although the gain depends on the event.

\section{Applications}
\paragraph{Network reliability.}
Keys may represent component tolerances or activation requirements, while the threshold represents available resources. A sweep finds the bottleneck level at which terminals first connect.

\paragraph{Threshold-controlled security networks.}
Vertices may represent authorized relays and edges enabled channels. The joint theorem permits both controls to be studied without confusing their spaces. Increasing an inclusion threshold cannot revoke a previously available route within this model.

\paragraph{Sensitivity analysis.}
Shared keys ensure that changes arise from newly activated components rather than resampling noise. Objects entering at the first crossing level are natural candidates for pivotality analysis and Russo-type derivative formulas.

\paragraph{Visualization.}
A threshold slider over one key field produces a faithful growth process: components appear, clusters merge, and crossings persist. It sharply distinguishes monotone evolution from unrelated snapshots.

\section{Scope and limitations}
The core theorems neither assume nor establish randomness, uniformity, or independence. Those assumptions enter only in the probabilistic specialization.

Monotonicity does not imply strictness. Finite crossing persistence is not an infinite-volume phase-transition theorem. Exact infinite-lattice thresholds require infinite configurations, limiting arguments, and model-specific tools such as planar duality and sharp-threshold theory. Site and bond models remain distinct despite their parallel order structure. Finally, applications to cryptography require explicit adversarial models and security definitions beyond graph connectivity.

\section{Future research}
Immediate directions are:
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*]
\item Develop the complete finite distribution under independent uniform keys, including $p^{N_1}(1-p)^{N_0}$ and its bond analogue.
\item Systematically derive nondecreasing finite-grid crossing probabilities from pointwise coupling and expectation.
\item Prove the finite Russo formula: for an increasing Boolean event and $p\in(0,1)$, the derivative of its Bernoulli probability polynomial equals the sum of pivotal probabilities.
\item For $n\ge2$ and $0<p<q<1$, prove strict increase of the $n\times n$ horizontal crossing probability.
\item Extend the simultaneous uniform-key realization to every increasing bond event on every finite graph.
\end{enumerate}
Beyond these finite steps lie planar primal--dual alternatives, matching-lattice transformations, exhaustion and compactness arguments, sharp thresholds, and infinite-cluster criteria.

\section{Conclusion}
A common-key threshold coupling replaces separately sampled configurations by one nested family. Open site and bond sets grow pointwise; witness paths persist; finite-grid crossings cannot disappear; and site and bond systems can advance together without being identified. Under independent uniform keys, all Bernoulli parameters live on one probability space, and increasing-event probabilities are monotone by averaging a pointwise inequality. Sorted activation and disjoint-set updates make the same idea computationally efficient. The construction cleanly separates conclusions due to deterministic order from those that still require probability, geometry, or infinite-volume analysis.

\end{document}

